#: ../../math.rst:2
msgid "Hermes concepts and equations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:4
msgid ""
"This page is the compact mathematical map behind the task guides. It "
"follows the notation of the Hermes paper while naming the corresponding "
"PyHermes objects explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:9
msgid "From a catalogue to a continuous field"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:11
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"For particle positions :math:`\\mathbf{x}_i`, catalogue weights "
":math:`w_i`, and optional particle-carried values :math:`q_i`, define"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:14
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"F(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_i w_i q_i\\,\n"
"\\delta_{\\mathrm D}^{3}(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}_i)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:19
msgid ""
"``catalog_weight`` supplies :math:`w_i`; ``field_value`` supplies "
":math:`q_i`. Unit values produce an ordinary number-density field. Mass, "
"velocity components, marks, or other scalar values produce weighted "
"fields without changing the projection algorithm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:24
msgid ""
"At multiresolution level :math:`J`, PyHermes projects the catalogue onto "
"a tensor-product scaling-function basis,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:27
msgid ""
"F_J(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{\\mathbf{l}}\n"
"\\epsilon_{J\\mathbf{l}}\\,\\phi_{J\\mathbf{l}}(\\mathbf{x}),\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"\\epsilon_{J\\mathbf{l}} = \\int F(\\mathbf{x})\n"
"\\phi_{J\\mathbf{l}}(\\mathbf{x})\\,d^3x."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:35
msgid ""
"The array of :math:`\\epsilon_{J\\mathbf{l}}` is ``SFCField.epsilon``. "
"``SFCProjection`` computes it directly from particle positions using the "
"compact support of the scaling function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:43
msgid ""
"Multiresolution field reconstruction in the Hermes paper: a discrete "
"catalogue is represented by compact scaling-function coefficients and may"
" then be evaluated as a continuous field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:48
msgid "Resolution and the role of J"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:50
msgid "The MRA grid has"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:52
msgid ""
"L = 2^J, \\qquad N_{\\mathrm{MRA}} = L^3 = 2^{3J},\n"
"\\qquad \\Delta x = \\frac{L_{\\mathrm{box}}}{2^J}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:57
msgid ""
"Increasing ``J`` by one halves the nominal cell size and multiplies the "
"number of three-dimensional coefficients by eight. The scaling functions "
"are not nearest-grid-point cells, but :math:`\\Delta x` remains the "
"useful first check: measurements at separations or bin widths comparable "
"to it require a convergence test."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:63
msgid ""
"The visual :math:`J=7,8,9` reconstruction comparison and practical "
"resolution guidance are kept with the projection workflow in "
":doc:`get_start/sfc_projection/sfc_projection`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:68
msgid "Normalisation carried by SFCField"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:70
msgid "Projection weights are divided according to ``weight_normalization``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:72
msgid "``raw``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:73
msgid "No division. The field integral is :math:`\\sum_i w_i q_i`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:75
msgid "``catalog``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:76
msgid ""
"Divide by :math:`\\sum_i w_i`. For an unweighted number field the field "
"integral is one. This is the default for catalogue statistics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:79
msgid "``field``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:80
msgid "Divide by :math:`\\sum_i w_i q_i`, giving a unit-integral weighted field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:82
msgid ""
"``unit`` is accepted by high-level tasks as a request to convert each "
"input field to unit integral. On an ``SFCField`` itself, use "
"``to_unit_weight()`` or ``with_normalization(\"unit\")``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:86
msgid ""
"``field_mean_density(value_unit=\"grid\")`` divides the field integral by"
" ``L**3``. ``value_unit=\"physical\"`` divides by ``box_size**3``. The "
"same choice is available when evaluating the continuous field with "
"``field_density_at_pos``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:92
msgid "Window projection and convolution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:94
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Let :math:`W(\\mathbf{x})` be a translation-invariant window and "
":math:`\\widehat W(\\mathbf{k})` its Fourier transform. Projection onto "
"the same scaling-function space introduces the autocorrelation factor "
":math:`\\widehat\\Phi`, so the stored discrete kernel is schematically"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:99
#, python-brace-format
msgid "K_W(\\mathbf{k}) = \\widehat W(\\mathbf{k})\\widehat\\Phi(\\mathbf{k})."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:103
msgid "Applying a window is then"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:105
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\\epsilon_W = \\operatorname{iFT}\n"
"\\left[\\operatorname{FT}(\\epsilon)K_W\\right]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:110
msgid "In Python this is simply:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:116
msgid ""
"The output is another ``SFCField``. Field addition and subtraction "
"combine coefficient arrays; field multiplication forms a coefficient-"
"space product:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:125
msgid ""
"The scaling-function connection relations make these coefficient-space "
"products the MRA representation of the spatial products used by Hermes "
"estimators."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:130
msgid "One-point statistics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:132
msgid ""
"A smoothing window :math:`W_R` gives a local field :math:`F_R = F * W_R`."
" ``Counting`` samples this continuous filtered field at uniform random "
"positions. Histograms, moments, and one-point PDFs are ordinary post-"
"processing of the returned samples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:137
msgid "The one-field second moment can also be written as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:139
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\\sigma_W^2(R) = \\left\\langle \\delta_W^2(\\mathbf{x})\\right\\rangle,\n"
"\\qquad \\delta_W = \\delta * W_R."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:144
msgid ""
"Changing the window changes the statistic: ``sphere`` and ``gaussian`` "
"are low-pass filters; ``cw``, ``cws``, and ``gdw`` are localised high-"
"pass or band-pass filters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:149
msgid "Two-point statistics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:151
msgid ""
"Let :math:`D` and :math:`R` denote consistently normalised data and "
"random fields, and :math:`\\Delta = D-R`. For a separation-binning window"
" :math:`W_b`, PyHermes evaluates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:155
msgid ""
"\\Delta DD[W_b] =\n"
"\\left\\langle (\\Delta_1 * W_b)\\,\\Delta_2\\right\\rangle,\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"RR[W_b] =\n"
"\\left\\langle (R_1 * W_b)\\,R_2\\right\\rangle,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:163
msgid "and returns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:165
#, python-brace-format
msgid "\\xi[W_b] = \\frac{\\Delta DD[W_b]}{RR[W_b]}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:169
msgid ""
"This is the field form of the Landy--Szalay combination. The task may "
"also return ``dd``, ``dr``, ``rd``, ``delta_dd``, and ``rr`` separately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:172
msgid "The geometry resides in :math:`W_b`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:174
msgid "``shell`` gives an isotropic separation;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:175
msgid "``thick_shell`` and ``gaussian_shell`` average over a finite radial range;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:176
msgid "``ring`` maps :math:`(s,\\mu)` into transverse and line-of-sight offsets;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:177
msgid ""
"``disk``, ``cylshell``, and ``cylinder`` define alternative anisotropic "
"averages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:180
msgid ""
"Additional ``window1`` and ``window2`` filters act on the two input "
"vertices before the binning window is applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:184
msgid "Standard three-point statistics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:186
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``Corr_3PCF`` performs a Monte Carlo translational and rotational average"
" for a triangle with fixed :math:`r_{12}`, :math:`r_{13}`, and sampled "
"included angle. Its three input vertices may have independent smoothing "
"windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:190
msgid ""
"``center=\"particle\"`` uses catalogue objects as primary vertices. This "
"is the dual-sphere construction in the paper. ``center=\"box_random\"`` "
"samples primary vertices uniformly in the volume, corresponding to the "
"triplet-sphere construction. ``n_rot`` controls the random rotational "
"average around each centre."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:196
msgid ""
"The connected three-point statistic is assembled from data and random "
"field products. ``zeta`` is the connected 3PCF and ``Q`` is the reduced "
"3PCF,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:199
msgid ""
"Q = \\frac{\\zeta}\n"
"{\\xi_{12}\\xi_{13}+\\xi_{12}\\xi_{23}+\\xi_{13}\\xi_{23}}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:205
msgid "3PCF multipoles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:207
msgid "The angular dependence of the 3PCF can be expanded as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:209
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\\zeta(r_{12},r_{13},\\mu)\n"
"= \\sum_{\\ell}(2\\ell+1)\\,\n"
"\\zeta_\\ell(r_{12},r_{13})P_\\ell(\\mu)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:215
msgid "For a thin shell, the Fourier-space multipole window has the form"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:217
msgid ""
"W_{r}^{\\ell m}(\\mathbf{k}) =\n"
"4\\pi i^\\ell j_\\ell(2\\pi kr)Y_\\ell^m(\\widehat{\\mathbf{k}})."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:222
msgid ""
"``Corr_3PCF_Multipole`` constructs the required window-filtered "
":math:`(\\ell,m)` fields and contracts their local products. Only "
":math:`m\\geq0` fields are built explicitly; negative modes follow from "
"spherical-harmonic conjugation symmetry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:227
msgid ""
"The two radial supports are supplied by ``binning_window12`` and "
"``binning_window13``. Thin shells use the analytic spherical-Bessel "
"response. General radial profiles use :math:`U_\\ell(k)Y_\\ell^m` "
"kernels, with numerical spherical-Hankel tabulation when no direct "
"analytic higher-order response is available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:234
msgid "Weighted and derived physical fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:236
msgid ""
"Weights modify the field before projection; windows modify it afterwards."
" This separation makes marked and physical-field statistics natural:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:239
msgid ""
"F_q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_i\n"
"w_i^{\\mathrm c}w_i^{\\mathrm{opt}}m_iq_i\n"
"\\delta_{\\mathrm D}^{3}(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}_i)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:245
msgid ""
"Factorisable catalogue weights belong in ``catalog_weight`` or "
"``field_value``. A separation-dependent factor may belong in a window. "
"General non-separable pair weights do not preserve one translation-"
"invariant convolution and require a decomposition or explicit correction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:250
msgid "Operator windows use the Fourier identities"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:252
msgid ""
"\\widehat{\\partial_{\\hat n}f}=i(\\mathbf{k}\\cdot\\hat{\\mathbf "
"n})\\hat f,\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"\\widehat{\\nabla^2 f}=-k^2\\hat f,\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"\\widehat{\\nabla^{-2}f}=-\\frac{1}{k^2}\\hat f."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:260
msgid ""
"The inverse-Laplacian zero mode is set to zero, fixing the arbitrary "
"additive constant of the potential. Cosmological prefactors are "
"intentionally applied outside that mathematical operator, where "
"coordinate and unit conventions are explicit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:266
msgid "Projection and repeated windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:268
msgid ""
"The finite scaling-function projection matters when operations are "
"composed. A ``WindowFunc`` stores :math:`K_W`, not a bare "
":math:`\\widehat W`. Therefore:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:272
msgid "``field @ W1 @ W2`` applies two projected kernels;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:273
msgid "``W1 * W2`` multiplies two already projected kernels point by point;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:274
msgid ""
"neither expression is, in general, identical to projecting the continuous"
" product :math:`\\widehat W_1\\widehat W_2` exactly once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../math.rst:277
msgid ""
"For a production composite window, the cleanest current route is to "
"define the complete Fourier expression in one built-in or custom window "
"and apply it once. ``W1 * W2`` remains useful for exploratory projected-"
"kernel composition and for testing separable constructions, provided this"
" distinction is kept in mind. See :doc:`windows` for code and kernel-mode"
" details."
msgstr ""
